MediaCodec VP8: a refused picture must take the decoder's reference with it

A three-party mesh showed one remote tile as persistent macroblock rubble -- large flat
blocks, ghosts of older frames, colour smeared across them -- for minutes at a time, while
the other remote tile on the same handset stayed clean and the audio was unaffected. Every
counter a frozen or black tile would trip read healthy: RTP arriving, the jitter buffer
draining, decode fps non-zero, frames reaching the renderer. Measured 2026-09-30.

The cause is one flag that is set and never cleared.

decode_vpx is careful never to hand the component a partial VP8 picture. A missing frame
start, an interior RTP hole, a tail short of the first partition the frame tag declares, a
packet that could not be taken into the input buffer, a picture larger than the buffer --
each of those drops the picture entire, which is right, because half a picture decodes to
nothing good.

What it did next was feed in the following picture.

The decoder's reference is a chain. The picture that was refused is the one the NEXT
inter-frame is coded against, so the inter-frame after a drop is a difference against a
frame the component does not hold. It does not refuse that: it decodes it, returns a
picture, and the error propagates into every inter-frame after it until a keyframe arrives.
Nothing required one. `dec_seen_keyframe` was set by the first keyframe of a stream and
never cleared again for the life of the decoder, so the rule it exists for -- no
inter-frames without a reference -- was enforced exactly once, at decoder start, and never
at the moment a loss actually needed it.

So the fix is to make the start rule the recovery rule: clear the flag wherever a picture
is refused, and let the checks that already exist do the rest.

  * Every refusal path calls vp8_reference_lost(). It is idempotent, so a burst of loss
    counts as one break, and it records the moment of the break rather than the last
    picture to notice it.
  * While the flag is false the existing test drops dependent inter-frames -- the same code
    that protected a freshly created decoder, now reached after a loss as well.
  * vp8_reference_regained() sets it again, and is reached only through the `sized_ok`
    gate: a keyframe that itself lost packets is rejected by the completeness checks before
    it can end a recovery, so recovery ends on a keyframe that was actually decodable.
  * It reports how long the stream went without one, because the bound is the thing worth
    knowing.

Nothing here sends a request. A picture-less decode already publishes
PJMEDIA_EVENT_KEYFRAME_MISSING, `vid_stream` already turns that into an RTCP-FB PLI bounded
to two packets per event and rate limited to one transmission per PJMEDIA_RTCP_FB_INTERVAL
(50 ms), and the sender rate limits keyframes with
PJMEDIA_VID_STREAM_MIN_KEYFRAME_INTERVAL_MSEC (1000 ms). A request path here would be a
second one.

Nor does recovery depend on the PLI arriving at all, which matters because the deployment
puts a B2BUA in the media path. The encoder is configured with `i-frame-interval` =
KEYFRAME_INTERVAL = 1 second, so a keyframe is coming regardless; the PLI only makes it
sooner. The worst case is a tile holding its last good picture for about a second, instead
of decoding rubble indefinitely.

Measured on an SM-M146B over Wi-Fi after the change, three natural loss events in one call:

    11:34:53.766  Dropped picture: could not take packet 0 of 3 into the decoder input
                  buffer | complete=617 drop-head=0 drop-holes=1
    11:34:53.766  Reference lost: a packet never reached the decoder input buffer |
                  losses=1 recoveries=0
    11:34:53.766  Dropped picture: decoder has no trusted reference and this is an
                  inter-frame (3488 bytes); waiting for a keyframe
    11:34:54.851  Decoder reference regained: keyframe of 2904 bytes after 1084 ms without
                  one | losses=1 recoveries=1 inter-frames withheld=13
    11:34:58.503  Decoder reference regained: keyframe of 2887 bytes after 458 ms
    11:35:22.520  Decoder reference regained: keyframe of 2849 bytes after 587 ms

losses equals recoveries at every point, recovery is 458-1084 ms, and it takes no hangup,
no video toggle, no hold and no rebuild of the conference.

The Phase 3 start rule is unchanged and still load bearing: a decoder recreated by a video
off/on still refuses inter-frames until its first keyframe, which is what stopped
c2.android.vp8.decoder wedging at C2_CORRUPTED (2026-09-26). The counters reset with the
component, because a recreation is a new chain rather than a continuation of the old one.

